# Allocation Discipline

Our games are GC-constrained (WebGL). Every managed allocation in a per-frame or per-message path is a future GC spike. Minimize allocations; justify any you add.

## Closures and delegates

A lambda capturing a local or `this` allocates a closure object **every call**.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — closure allocated per call
button.onClick.AddListener(() => Fire(currentTarget));
units.Sort((a, b) => a.Score.CompareTo(b.Score));

// ✅ GOOD — cached delegate, no capture
private static readonly Comparison<Unit> ByScore = (a, b) => a.Score.CompareTo(b.Score);
units.Sort(ByScore);

// ✅ GOOD — method group registered once in Awake, unregistered in OnDestroy
button.onClick.AddListener(OnFireClicked);
```

- Cache comparison/callback delegates in `static readonly` fields when they capture nothing.
- Register listeners once (Awake/Start), not per frame.

## Boxing

Boxing allocates every time a value type becomes `object` or an interface.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — boxes
object key = someEnum;                        // enum → object
Debug.Log("Count: " + count);                 // int → object via concat
IComparable c = myStruct;                     // struct → interface
string.Format("{0}", score);                  // int → object

// ✅ GOOD
Debug.Log($"Count: {count.ToString()}");      // still allocates the string — keep out of hot paths entirely
EqualityComparer<MyEnum>.Default.Equals(a, b); // no boxing for enum comparison in generics
```

- Never use enums or structs as `Dictionary` keys without an `IEqualityComparer` (default comparer boxes on older runtimes) — or use `int` keys.
- Don't pass structs through `object`/interface parameters in hot code.

## `params` and strings

- `params T[]` allocates an array per call — use explicit overloads for 1–3 args in hot paths.
- No string concatenation, `string.Format`, or `$"..."` interpolation in per-frame code. Build UI strings only when the value **changes**, not every frame.

## Collections

- Reuse collections: allocate once, `Clear()` per use. No `new List<T>()` inside `Update` or per-message handlers.
- Pre-size collections when the count is known: `new List<T>(64)`, `new Dictionary<K,V>(capacity)`.
- Prefer `foreach` only on arrays and `List<T>` (struct enumerators, no alloc); `foreach` over an interface reference (`IEnumerable<T>`) boxes the enumerator — use `for`.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No capturing lambdas in per-frame or per-message code; delegates cached
- [ ] No boxing: no struct/enum → object/interface conversions in hot paths
- [ ] No `params` calls, string concat, or interpolation in per-frame code
- [ ] Collections reused with `Clear()`, pre-sized where count is known
- [ ] Every new allocation in gameplay code is justified in the change description
